  "cues": {
    "mountain": {
      "start": "Mountain ultras are won on the uphills and kept on the descents. Start conservatively, the grade is steeper than you think, and the altitude (if present) compounds aerobic demand. Settle into a hiking rhythm on major climbs from km 1.",
      "pacing": "Even pacing means your perceived effort stays consistent. Climb at RPE 5-6, descent at RPE 5-6. You will run slower on climbs and faster on descents, and that's correct. The pacing table scales each segment by its terrain difficulty; trust that allocation.",
      "challenges": "Relentless climbing in the first third of the race, switchback-dense ascents that break rhythm, altitude if applicable, exposed ridge sections in wind, long runnable flats that feel 'fast' but are deceptive energy drains.",
      "mistakes": "Climbing at threshold instead of sustainable effort, leads to bonking by km 30-40. Descending too fast early, banking time you need later. Forgetting to fuel and hydrate on climbs (easy to skip when working hard)."
    },
    "technical": {
      "start": "Technical terrain demands mental focus before muscle focus. Many athletes start fast on rocky ground and tire their brain before their legs. Your first 5km should be about footfall rhythm and line choice, not pace. Settle into the rock.",
      "pacing": "Even pacing on technical terrain is about consistent confidence, not consistent speed. Rock hopping at RPE 5-6 on the rocks; faster running on any smooth sections. Your pacing table allocates time for the technical sections; running them faster = more risk of a stumble that costs you 30 seconds and confidence.",
      "challenges": "Boulder fields and scree, rooty forest where every step is variable, steep rocky descents requiring hands-on scrambling, exposure (cliffsides, narrow ridges), sections that are slow and require navigation concentration.",
      "mistakes": "Running technical sections at threshold to 'make time', a fall or twisted ankle ends the race. Nervous footfalls that slow you more than confident hiking would. Ignoring the pacing table's technical-segment allowance and cramping later when you realize you over-extended."
    },
    "alpine": {
      "start": "Alpine races often start with a long climb or exposed ridge run. Your goal finish time assumes a steady effort, not speed chasing. The first km sets the tone: run 30 seconds slower than race pace to settle into a sustainable rhythm on steep ground. Alpine terrain punishes hard starts with calf and quad fatigue mid-race.",
      "pacing": "Even pacing on alpine terrain means matching your effort to the gradient, not your speed. A runnable 5:00/km climb should feel the same effort as a 3:00/km descent. Use RPE (Rate of Perceived Exertion): aim for RPE 5-6 on climbs, RPE 5-6 on descents. This feels 'steady' across all gradients.",
      "challenges": "Exposed ridges in wind, boulder fields requiring hands-on scrambling, sudden weather changes (temperature, visibility), long climbs that expose fitness gaps early, steep descents testing eccentric leg strength and confidence on loose rock.",
      "mistakes": "Pushing climbs too hard in the first half, trying to match your goal pace instead of your goal effort. Overriding caution on technical descents and catching toe. Walking every steep climb instead of power-hiking (which costs more time than you save)."
    },
    "trail": {
      "start": "Trail ultras sit between 'mountain' and 'runnable', expect rooty climbs, dirt descents, and sections of pure trail that feel easy until they bite your quads. Start steady and let the terrain speak. The first km is about settling into a rhythm, not testing your pace.",
      "pacing": "Even pacing on trail means you maintain the same effort across varied terrain: runnable flats at RPE 5-6, climbs at RPE 5-6, descents at RPE 5-6. Your speed changes; your effort doesn't. The pacing table is built to match this.",
      "challenges": "Rooty forest sections where every step varies, rolling terrain that tricks you into running too hard on 'gentle' sections, tree roots and rocks hidden by leaves (especially in wet conditions), descents that are fast but require confidence on uneven ground.",
      "mistakes": "Overrunning the 'easy' rolling sections and running out of legs mid-race. Underestimating the quads damage of downhill running and bonking in the final stages. Not eating enough on the 'easy' runnable sections because you don't feel the effort."
    },
    "generic": {
      "start": "For races without detailed course data, assume a 'mixed terrain' pacing model: expect rolling terrain with varied climbing and descent, some technical sections, some runnable flats. Start conservatively and let the first 10km show you the true character of the course.",
      "pacing": "Even pacing means maintaining RPE 5-6 across all terrain changes. Slow down on climbs, speed up on descents; the pacing model (finish time spread across distance) assumes this. Trust the allocation.",
      "challenges": "Varied terrain (climbs, technical sections, flat sections), potential for unexpected navigation, weather exposure, fatigue from repetitive impact or technical focus.",
      "mistakes": "Pushing harder on the 'easy' sections to make time. Running technical sections at threshold. Neglecting hydration and fuel because you're not sure how much energy the race will demand."
    },
    "road": {
      "start": "Road marathons reward consistency and penalise ego. The pace will feel easy for the first 10km, resist pushing harder. Road running is repetitive; your quads will tire from the impact, and your aerobic system can't spare the watts. Start at your goal pace and commit to steady effort.",
      "pacing": "Even pacing on road is close to actual consistent pace (unlike trail). Aim for RPE 5-6 for the full distance. Your pacing table is your pace chart, run it. Any deviation up in the first half costs you in the final 10km.",
      "challenges": "Quad fatigue from repetitive impact, mental monotony (road doesn't change), wind on exposed sections, a 'wall' at km 35+ if you went too hard early, the subtle slope you didn't notice on the reconnaissance.",
      "mistakes": "Starting too fast because road running feels easier than trail. Pacing the early miles at threshold effort (RPE 6-7) instead of RPE 5-6, banking time that you can't spend later. Forgetting hydration on road because aid stations feel infrequent."
    }
